# TypeScript libraries

Application code imports runtime classes from `@oatscenter/trellis` and the `apis`
and `participants` namespaces from its ordinary generated package. With
`[package]` and `[generate.typescript].output` in `trellis.toml`:

```ts
import { TrellisClient } from "@oatscenter/trellis";
import { participants } from "orders-trellis";

const client = await TrellisClient.connect({
  participant: participants.ordersApp.participant,
  trellisUrl: "https://trellis.example.com",
  auth: "browser",
}).orThrow();
```

Do not construct API or participant metadata in TypeScript. Author it in native
IDL and run `trellis generate`. Configure output with
`[generate.typescript].output`. Each language gets one package, not one per API
or participant.

The generated ESM package contains executable `.js`, `.d.ts`, and `package.json`,
at the source package version, with the published runtime dependency matching the CLI.
It needs no consumer transpiler or Trellis CLI. Install it with ordinary package
tooling, for example `npm install ./trellis`; Deno projects can map the package
name to `./trellis/index.js` in their own imports. Generation creates no Deno
configuration and never edits the consumer's manifest. Commit output when clones
should build without regeneration or the Trellis source-package cache.

Use `@oatscenter/trellis/service` for service bootstrap in Node or Deno.
Pass `participant` and the immutable provisioned identity `seed` to
`TrellisService.connect(...)`; `name` is optional. Automatic telemetry can be
disabled with `telemetry: false`, while `runtime` configures service logging,
default request timeout, event stream selection, no-responder retry, and health
heartbeat interval. Use
the connected service's generated handlers and `kv`, `store`, and `jobs` handles.
The browser-safe root does not expose native filesystem helpers.

Connected clients expose only the actions selected by their generated
participant. RPCs return `Result`-style values; `.orThrow()` is convenient at an
application boundary, while handlers should model expected failures explicitly.
Do not import provider implementation source to call a service: depend on its
source package, select exact actions in native IDL, and generate your local SDK.

Find exact signatures through [/api](/api). Generate after every IDL change
before checking extracted handler types.

Generated paginated RPCs retain their one-page method and add lazy `.pages(...)`
and `.items(...)` iterators. Missing `nextCursor` is the only end signal;
pagination is keyset-based and does not generally promise a snapshot.

State is one typed value per declared resource. KV is typed key/value history,
and Store remains raw object bytes. Optional resources are `undefined`; an
available but unwritten State resource returns no value. State/KV representation
migrations happen in SDK memory and do not rewrite stored bytes.

Generated service participants expose declared jobs through `service.jobs`.
Keyed capacity waits happen before handler execution and do not consume retry
attempts; retryable handler errors do. See [Jobs: TypeScript](/guides/using-jobs-ts).

## Transport upgrade notifications

A routine authorization refresh promotes the refreshed context in place on the
existing physical attachment; it does not reconnect a healthy socket. When the
newer authority safely covers the policy already admitted but offers additional
transport capability, the connection reports a retained
`transportUpgradeAvailable` observation instead of adopting it, and application
code decides whether and when to pay for the wider policy:

```ts
const client = await TrellisClient.connect({
  participant: participants.ordersApp.participant,
  trellisUrl: "https://trellis.example.com",
  auth: "browser",
}).orThrow();

const stop = client.connection.subscribe((status) => {
  if (status.phase === "connected" && status.transportUpgradeAvailable) {
    console.log("A wider transport policy is available to adopt.");
  }
});

// ... later, when the application accepts a real reconnect:
try {
  await client.connection.refreshTransport().orThrow();
} catch (error) {
  // connection_closed | authorization_unavailable | timeout | transport_error
  console.warn("transport refresh failed", error);
}

stop();
```

`transportUpgradeAvailable` is a retained observation, not a phase: a disconnect
clears it, and a successful replacement recomputes it from the actual admitted
policy. A listener added later immediately receives the current retained status,
so late subscribers do not miss an outstanding upgrade.

`refreshTransport()` performs a real physical reconnect, so it can interrupt
in-flight RPCs and ephemeral Live and Operation observations. Reconnecting
automatically whenever an upgrade appears is not the default integration
pattern; adopt the wider policy only when the application can tolerate the
interruption, or let an otherwise necessary reconnect adopt it implicitly.

A connected physical socket does not by itself mean the current application
authorization is available. When a call needs authority the admitted attachment
has not installed, it fails with the transport-class
`transport_upgrade_required` error rather than an authorization denial: the
credential is valid and the attachment has simply not adopted it. Call
`refreshTransport()` and retry. The Rust client exposes the equivalent retained
status and explicit refresh capability.
